Exactly. That’s the practical core of skill acquisition.

permission to try
→ beginner reps
→ visible mistakes
→ feedback
→ adjustment
→ more reps
→ competence
→ confidence

The embarrassment piece matters because shame can interrupt the loop before learning compounds. If every mistake gets interpreted as “evidence I’m bad at this,” practice dies early. If it gets interpreted as error signal, the same mistake becomes useful.

So a good training rule is:

Protect the learner, not the error.

Let yourself be clumsy. Keep the feedback honest. Fix the mistake. Repeat.

That is basically Kintsugi applied to competence:

failure → information → repair → stronger next attempt

And once you internalize that, “talent” starts looking less mystical. A lot of capability is just sustained willingness to remain bad long enough to become good.